        "title": "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "93c18ea6-97e8-5398-8c5a-d115930d1557",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "Given $f(x)=\\frac{e^{x}}{2}$ and $h(x)=- 4 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\sinx.$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})&=\\frac{u^{\\prime}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot v^{\\prime}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(\\frac{e^{x}}{2})^{\\prime}\\cdot (- 4 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})-(\\frac{e^{x}}{2})\\cdot (- 4 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})^{\\prime}}{(- 4 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\sinx)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\cosx)}{16\\sin^{2}x}\\\\ &=\\frac{2{e}^{x}(\\cosx-\\sinx)}{16\\sin^{2}x}\\\\ &=\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\cosx-\\sinx)}{8\\sin^{2}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "3fe9e331-8ba0-5646-9387-65ecbc89fb14",
            ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
            ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
